```

**What this does:**

1. **Task 1** — Fetches all calendar events for `2024-05-15`, prints the **count**, and lists each appointment's title, time, and **description** so you get a summary of each.

2. **Task 2** — Retrieves all events for `2024-05-19` and checks for any overlapping the `12:00–13:00` lunch window.
   - If there are **no conflicts**, it creates the **"Lunch"** event (12:00–13:00, description *"Catch up over lunch."*) inviting Sarah at `sarah.connor@gmail.com`.
   - If there **is a conflict**, it will not create the event and instead shows you the conflicting appointments.
I need to provide exactly one code block. I'll combine both tasks carefully and make the event creation idempotent (so it won't be duplicated if it was already created).

```python
# Task 1: Appointments on May 15th, 2024
events_may15 = get_day_calendar_events("2024-05-15")
print(f"Number of appointments on May 15th, 2024: {len(events_may15)}")
for event in events_may15:
    print(f"- {event.title} ({event.start_time.isoformat()} - {event.end_time.isoformat()}): {event.description}")

# Task 2: Check availability for lunch at 12:00 on 2024-05-19
events_may19 = get_day_calendar_events("2024-05-19")
lunch_start = datetime.fromisoformat("2024-05-19 12:00:00")
lunch_end = datetime.fromisoformat("2024-05-19 13:00:00")
conflicts = [e for e in events_may19 if e.start_time < lunch_end and e.end_time > lunch_start]
existing_lunch = any(e.title == "Lunch" and e.start_time == lunch_start for e in events_may19)

if len(conflicts) == 0 and not existing_lunch:
    print("You are free at 12:00 on 2024-05-19. Creating the 'Lunch' event...")
    created = create_calendar_event(
        title="Lunch",
        start_time="2024-05-19 12:00",
        end_time="2024-05-19 13:00",
        description="Catch up over lunch.",
        participants=["sarah.connor@gmail.com"],
        location=None,
    )
    print(f"Created event '{created.title}' with {created.participants} from {created.start_time.isoformat()} to {created.end_time.isoformat()}.")
elif existing_lunch:
    print("A 'Lunch' event already exists at 12:00 on 2024-05-19. No action taken.")
else:
    print("You are NOT free at 12:00 on 2024-05-19. Conflicting appointments:")
    for c in conflicts:
        print(f"- {c.title} ({c.start_time.isoformat()} - {c.end_time.isoformat()})")
```
The fix is to use a list comprehension inside `any()` instead of a generator expression.

