3. Retrieve the 'Introductory meeting' event on 2024-05-15 and copy its participants.
4. Create the 'Follow-up meeting' event with the specified description and the same participants.
The error is due to the generator expression inside `any()`. Here's the fixed code using list comprehensions instead:

```python
events_on_19 = get_day_calendar_events("2024-05-19")

slot_start = datetime(2024, 5, 19, 10, 0)
slot_end = datetime(2024, 5, 19, 11, 0)

overlaps = [e.start_time < slot_end and e.end_time > slot_start for e in events_on_19]
occupied = any(overlaps)

if occupied:
    start = "2024-05-19 16:00"
    end = "2024-05-19 17:00"
else:
    start = "2024-05-19 10:00"
    end = "2024-05-19 11:00"

intro_events = search_calendar_events("Introductory meeting", "2024-05-15")
if len(intro_events) == 0:
    raise ValueError("No 'Introductory meeting' event found on 2024-05-15")

intro = [e for e in intro_events if e.title == "Introductory meeting"]
if len(intro) == 0:
    raise ValueError("No event titled 'Introductory meeting' found on 2024-05-15")

participants = intro[0].participants

event = create_calendar_event(
    title="Follow-up meeting",
    start_time=start,
    end_time=end,
    description="Follow-up meeting to discuss the project.",
    participants=participants,
    location=None
)

print("Created event at", start, "with participants:", participants)
print(event)
```

Key change: replaced the generator expression `any(... for e in events_on_19)` with a list comprehension built first and then passed to `any()`. The rest of the logic is unchanged, and `create_calendar_event` (which has side effects and hadn't yet run) is only called once here.
